## Kraken Exchange

### Current Features

+ REST Support
+ Websocket Support

### How to enable

+ [Enable via configuration]({{.RepoRoot}}/config/README.md#enable-exchange-via-config-example)

+ Individual package example below:

```go
    // Exchanges will be abstracted out in further updates and examples will be
    // supplied then
```

### How to do REST public/private calls

+ If enabled via "configuration".json file the exchange will be added to the
IBotExchange array in the ```go var bot Bot``` and you will only be able to use
the wrapper interface functions for accessing exchange data. View routines.go
for an example of integration usage with GoCryptoTrader. Rudimentary example
below:

main.go

```go
var k exchange.IBotExchange

for i := range bot.Exchanges {
    if bot.Exchanges[i].GetName() == "Kraken" {
        k = bot.Exchanges[i]
    }
}

// Public calls - wrapper functions

// Fetches current ticker information
tick, err := k.UpdateTicker(ctx, pair, asset.Spot)
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}

// Fetches current orderbook information
ob, err := k.UpdateOrderbook(ctx, pair, asset.Spot)
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}

// Private calls - wrapper functions - make sure your APIKEY and APISECRET are
// set and AuthenticatedAPISupport is set to true

// Fetches current account balances
balances, err := k.UpdateAccountBalances(ctx, asset.Spot)
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}
```

+ If enabled via individually importing package, rudimentary example below:

```go
// Public calls

// Fetches current ticker information
tickers, err := k.GetTickerInformation(ctx, &kraken.TickerInformationRequest{Pairs: currency.Pairs{pair}})
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}

// Fetches current orderbook information
books, err := k.GetOrderBook(ctx, &kraken.OrderBookRequest{Pair: pair})
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}

// Private calls - make sure your APIKEY and APISECRET are set and
// AuthenticatedAPISupport is set to true

// Fetches each asset's balance, with the amount held by open orders
balances, err := k.GetExtendedBalance(ctx, nil)
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}

// Submits an order to the exchange and returns its transaction ID
order, err := k.AddOrder(ctx, &kraken.AddOrderRequest{
    Pair: pair,
    Order: kraken.OrderParameters{
        OrderType: "limit",
        Side:      "buy",
        Volume:    0.001,
        Price:     kraken.OrderPrice{Value: 50000},
    },
})
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}
```

+ Derivatives (futures) calls are named after their section, such as GetFuturesTickers, SendFuturesOrder,
GetFuturesAccounts and the Derivatives History API's GetFuturesOrderEvents.

### How to do Websocket public/private calls

+ The spot websocket uses Kraken's websocket v2 API over three connections: market data subscriptions stream on the
public connection; account subscriptions stream, and orders are placed, on the private connection; and level 3 order
books stream on their own connection. The private and level 3 connections connect when their subscriptions are
configured, authenticated websocket support is enabled and credentials are set. Spot tickers carry no opening price
of the day, so they leave `Open` unset.

+ The futures websocket streams the tickers, order books and trades of futures subscriptions, and, when
authenticated websocket support is enabled and credentials are set, the account's open orders, fills and balances
with the `myOrders`, `myTrades` and `myWallet` channels. Futures order books stream whole, so futures subscriptions
take no `levels`. Futures order updates leave an order's average price, cost and fee unset, as the feeds cannot show
that the fills they report are all of the order's; each fill is relayed as the order's trade, with its price and fee.
Once a fill leaves nothing of an order remaining, the order is relayed filled, even if it filled while the futures
connection was down, if an update carrying its quantity was relayed earlier and the order is still tracked. When the
order's quantity is not known and its fills cannot make it up, as when it filled while the connection was down and no
report has given its quantity since, the update relaying it filled carries no quantities, as the order may have been
amended unseen, and the order manager takes all of the amount it holds as executed. An order no longer open stays
tracked until at least ten minutes pass without a report of it, an open orders snapshot that leaves out an open order
not yet filled in full counting as a report. One cancelled while the connection was down is not relayed cancelled, as
the open orders snapshot then only leaves it out, relaying nothing of it, which leaves its stored status as it was,
though its fills can still relay the status of a removal or rejection of it the open orders feed reported earlier. An
order that filled while the connection was down, whose quantity is not known and was never relayed, such as one placed
while it was down, gets from its fills the status the open orders feed's removal or rejection of it gives, if the order
is still tracked, such as closed for a stop removed as triggered, and otherwise none, as its fills cannot be told from
the fills of orders that finished before the feeds were subscribed, which are stored without a status for the order
manager to look up.

+ The private, level 3 and futures connections are optional: one that is refused, or drops, is dialled again on its
own while the other connections stay up. It waits the connection monitor delay, then twice as long after each further
failure, up to a minute, or the monitor delay when that is longer, and is dialled at the connection monitor's next
check once the wait has passed. Losing the public connection reconnects them all. While an optional connection is
down, the websocket still reads as connected, so the engine's sync manager keeps waiting on the websocket for the order
books it follows over that connection rather than reading them over REST, and those books keep their last state until
the connection is connected and subscribed again and new snapshots arrive.

+ Kraken's spot balance messages carry neither the amount on hold nor the credit it counts as available, so each one
the `myWallet` channel streams refreshes the stored spot balances over REST, at most once every five seconds, and the
balances are read a second time after a ledger update, as REST can lag it. An order the private connection reports
finished after fills, or cancelled or expired without saying how much of it filled, is likewise read over REST for its
fee, as execution reports carry each fill's fee alone, and so is a spot order cancelled over REST by its order ID while
the private connection is unavailable, which may not report the cancellation. These reads are made and retried only
while the websocket is connected, or still connecting.

+ The futures balances feed names single-collateral margin accounts differently from REST, so one is stored only
under the REST account it matches, such as `fi_xbtusd` for `F-XBT:USD`, once `UpdateAccountBalances` has stored that
account; other margin accounts follow REST. The multi-collateral wallet can be the account's unified wallet, whose
funds the spot balances list, and only the feed says whether it is: a balances snapshot carrying the wallet reports
it unified when `unified_balances` is true, and separate otherwise, while Kraken's futures REST reply does not say.
While spot is enabled, the `flex` wallet is stored empty until the feed reports it separate, as spot reads keep a
unified wallet's funds current, so they are counted once, under spot; with spot disabled they stay under futures. The
feed is the futures `myWallet` subscription, a default subscribed when authenticated websocket support is enabled and
credentials are set; without it, as with REST alone, a separate multi-collateral wallet's funds are not counted while
spot is enabled. Spot balances stored before spot was disabled stay stored, as any disabled asset's do.

+ A level 3 order book lists each order rather than the total at each price. It is subscribed to with the `allOrders`
channel at a depth of 10, 100 or 1000 price levels, for up to 200 pairs, the symbols Kraken allows on a connection:

```json
{"enabled": true, "channel": "allOrders", "asset": "spot", "levels": 100}
```

+ With the `orderbook` channel, the stored order book holds the total at each price. With the `allOrders` channel, it
holds a level for each order, so a price appears once for each order resting at it, and the level 3 book's snapshots
and updates are also sent to the data handler as `WebsocketLevel3Book`s with their order IDs. As a pair has one stored
order book, it can be subscribed to at one depth of either the `orderbook` or the `allOrders` channel.

+ Websocket v2 streams one candle interval for each pair on a connection, so a pair's candles can be subscribed to at
one interval: Kraken rejects a subscription to a second.

+ Default subscriptions are configured in the exchange's `features.subscriptions` config. Upgrading a config that
holds the previous default subscriptions adds the futures subscriptions to them; other subscriptions are kept as they
are. A subscription with `"asset": "all"` to a channel futures does not stream, which candles, the `allOrders` channel
and an order book at a depth are, is subscribed to for spot alone. So is one to a ticker, trades or order book that names
`pairs`, as a symbol of one asset is no symbol of the other, so a futures product is named with `"asset": "futures"`.

+ Kraken margins a spot order by its leverage and a futures order by the leverage preference set for its product, takes
no slippage tolerance, and the wrapper attaches no take profit, stop loss or stop entry to an order, so `SubmitOrder`
refuses `RiskManagementModes`, `SlippageTolerance` and any `MarginType` but `cash` on a spot order without leverage,
which is the order it places, rather than place the order without them. `ModifyOrder` refuses `RiskManagementModes`
and `SlippageTolerance` likewise, and a `NewClientOrderID`, which neither Kraken's amendments nor its edits take.

+ Orders can be managed over the private connection. The wrapper's spot order functions use it while it is connected
and holds a subscription, and REST otherwise, such as when Kraken refuses the websocket token:

```go
// Places an order over the private websocket connection
resp, err := k.WebsocketAddOrder(ctx, &kraken.WebsocketAddOrderRequest{
    Pair: pair,
    Order: kraken.WebsocketOrder{
        OrderType:  "limit",
        Side:       "buy",
        Quantity:   0.001,
        LimitPrice: 50000,
    },
})
if err != nil {
    // Handle error
}
```

